{
  "rules": [
    "Strong upward-trend underestimation: when the last 1–2 weeks show a sustained rise, the model systematically forecasts too low across the whole 30d horizon (level bias). Apply an upward level shift that grows with horizon.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias (increasing with time): errors are small in days 1–3, but become large from ~day 5 onward and very large by days 10–30. Use a ramping correction (e.g., add +X on day 1 increasing to +Y by day 30).",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: the model captures direction but reacts too slowly to accelerating rallies; it projects flat/sideways while realized prices continue climbing. Add a momentum term based on recent slope/returns to steepen the forecast path.",
    "Peak suppression / capped highs: during strong rallies the model’s forecast tops out around low-70s while realized moves into high-70s/80s. Remove implicit cap by scaling up positive trend regimes (multiplicative uplift on forecast changes).",
    "Underpricing after breakout days: following sharp up-moves, the model tends to revert toward the prior mean, but realized prices persist at the new higher regime. Reduce mean-reversion strength for 1–3 weeks after a breakout (regime persistence rule).",
    "Volatility underestimation: forecast path is too smooth versus realized; incorporate a volatility expansion so that in high-momentum regimes the forecast range/step sizes increase."
  ]
}
